
== mask ==
Patient <PERSON>, SSN XXXXXXX3456, has diabetes.

== redact vs replace ==
redact   Patient <PERSON>, SSN , has diabetes.
replace  Patient <PERSON>, SSN <SSN>, has diabetes.

== redaction destroys linkage ==
[('PERSON', 'John Smith', 0.85), ('PERSON', 'John Smith', 0.85), ('PERSON', 'John Smith', 0.85)]
[REDACTED] purchased a laptop on the 3rd.
[REDACTED] returned the laptop on the 9th.
[REDACTED] contacted support about a refund.

== encrypt, then decrypt ==
PERSON  MdiDKDGPEDxrHWGUXQZseSZiklSQbIVK3FeY88ZlpyE=   (ciphertext differs per run)
US_SSN  lfeAEx_xnNfGTT9u5w5agcsWoQFlU5IHp6UDHLRS1A0=   (ciphertext differs per run)
Patient John Smith, SSN 900-12-3456, has diabetes.

== encrypt the same SSN twice: no linkage ==
same ciphertext both times: False

== what the detector misses, every operator allows ==
[]
  at 0.5: My SSN is nine zero zero one two three four five six.
[('US_SSN', 0.4), ('US_PASSPORT', 0.05), ('US_BANK_NUMBER', 0.05), ('US_DRIVER_LICENSE', 0.01)]
  at 0.5: My SSN is 900123456.

== hash operator: default (random salt) vs fixed salt ==
default -> same hash both times: False
{'salt': 'a-fixed-16b-salt'} -> same hash both times: True

== unsalted SHA-256 of an SSN, attacked ==
found: 900-12-3456
rate: 1,148,002 hashes/sec
all 1,000,000,000: 14.5 minutes (extrapolated)

== the fix: a keyed hash (HMAC) keeps linkage, needs the key ==
same value, same keyed hash: True b895bf54c75443eb...
